 "_prompt": "You are matching code-review findings against a known bug. Reply with ONLY a JSON object.\n\nKNOWN BUG: When the target is itself a shadow host, its own shadow content is hidden.\n\nRules:\n- Answer \"yes\" only if the text describes THIS specific bug: the same faulty behaviour in the same situation. A finding about a nearby but different behaviour (another header, another edge case, a different race) is \"no\".\n- A finding that says the behaviour was checked and PASSED, or that the code handles it correctly, is \"no\".\n- Wording does not matter; the behaviour does.\n- For handoffs: count only text that tells the implementor to fix this bug (a reported finding, a fix instruction). Restated acceptance criteria that merely describe the desired behaviour do NOT count as carrying the finding unless the handoff ties them to a reported failure.\n\n=== REVIEW 1 FINDINGS ===\n- [blocking] `root` is not respected for enclosing shadow roots. `roots` pushes every shadow root enclosing a target and `walk()` covers the whole root, so siblings of `root` inside that shadow root get hidden. Repro: `sr` holds `outside=<button>` and `R=<div>` containing `t=<button>`; `ariaHideOutside([t], {root: R})` leaves `outside` with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. The light-DOM baseline (`root=R` in body) keeps the sibling visible, so this is shadow-only. Fix: clip enclosing shadow roots to `root`, or skip them when `root` is not an ancestor of the host path.\n- [blocking] A light-DOM child slotted into a target's shadow subtree is hidden. Repro: host `B` has light child `<button>`; `B.sr` has `<div id=pop><slot></slot></div>`; `ariaHideOutside([pop])` leaves the light button with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. It renders inside the target, so it breaks \"targets or their descendants stay visible\". Scope judgment: the criteria don't name slots explicitly.\n- [minor] With `root` set to a light container that does not contain the host, siblings inside the shadow root are still hidden. Arguably correct (the shadow root is not above `root`), but the docs don't say so.\n- [minor] A light child appended to a host-path element after the call is hidden. Consistent with the initial walk, and the same slot issue applies.\n- [minor] Not reproduced: the `keepVisible` probe passes once imported from `../src/ariaHideOutside`.\n- [minor] `FEATURE_FLOW.md` is untracked at the repo root. It was there before I started and is not part of this diff.\n=== REVIEW 2 FINDINGS ===\n- [blocking] A node appended inside a slotted light child of a shadow host on the path is hidden. The slotted child is kept visible by the new `assignedSlot` rule but is not in `visibleNodes` and is not an ancestor of any target, so the observer's `nodeContains` check does not skip its later children. Reproduced: `h.appendChild` of `<button id=b>` into a host with `<div id=pop><slot></slot></div>`, then `q(h,'#b').appendChild(<em>)`, then `expect(H(em)).toBe(false)` failed with `Received: true`. The same `em` present at call time stays visible (Q1 passed). Fix idea: register slotted-visible nodes in `visibleNodes`, or check `assignedSlot` for the change target in the observer too.\n- [minor] Changing a light child's `slot` attribute after the call does not re-evaluate it, so a child moved into the target's slot stays aria-hidden (reproduced: `c.removeAttribute('slot')` then `expect(H(c)).toBe(false)` got true). Not in the criteria, and the light DOM has no attribute observation either.\n- [minor] When a target is a light child of host B, B's own shadow siblings are not walked (B's shadow root does not enclose the target). This matches the criteria wording, but a web component's internal content stays exposed. Reproduced as designed, not a failure.\n- [minor] `shouldUseInert` could not be exercised: jsdom has no `HTMLElement.inert`, so `supportsInert` is false and the path is skipped. Not reproduced in this environment.\n=== IMPLEMENTOR HANDOFF 1 (sent after review 1) ===\nGOAL: Fix two reproduced bugs in the uncommitted shadow DOM support for `ariaHideOutside` (repo /tmp/ff-eval-bj4jzpw2/workspace; see `git diff` for the change so far: packages/@react-aria/overlays/src/ariaHideOutside.ts, plus tests at the end of packages/@react-aria/overlays/test/ariaHideOutside.test.js in `describe('ariaHideOutside with Shadow DOM')`).\n\nFILES: packages/@react-aria/overlays/src/ariaHideOutside.ts, packages/@react-aria/overlays/test/ariaHideOutside.test.js (add tests inside the existing shadow DOM describe, reusing its helpers).\n\nBugs (flag on):\n1. `root` is not honored. sr holds `outside=<button>` and `R=<div>` containing `t`; `ariaHideOutside([t], {root: R})` hides `outside`. Fix: only add an enclosing shadow root to `roots` when `root` contains its host (`nodeContains(root, shadowRoot.host)`), so shadow roots above `root` are skipped. `walk(root)` already covers root's own subtree. Test this case, plus a target in a shadow root nested inside R (that shadow root must still be walked, so its siblings get hidden).\n2. A light-DOM child slotted into a target is hidden. Host B has light child `<button id=slotted>`, B.shadowRoot has `<div id=pop><slot></slot></div>`, and `ariaHideOutside([pop])` puts aria-hidden on the light button even though it renders inside the target. Fix: in `acceptNode`, only when `shadowDOM()` is on, if `node.assignedSlot` is inside a visible node (`[...visibleNodes].some(v => nodeContains(v, node.assignedSlot))`), return FILTER_REJECT. Test that it covers both the initial walk and a slotted child appended to B later (MutationObserver path). If jsdom does not support `assignedSlot`, say so and test what you can.\n\nCONSTRAINTS: Keep the flag-off path behaviorally unchanged. Don't edit pre-existing tests, package.json or yarn.lock. No new deps.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA:\n1. With `shadowDOM()` on (`enableShadowDOM()` from `@react-stately/flags`), `ariaHideOutside(targets)` hides every element outside the targets, including sibling elements inside each target's own shadow root and inside every shadow root that encloses it (walking up via `getRootNode()`/`.host` to the document). Each shadow host on the path from the document to a target is NOT hidden, nor are the targets or their descendants.\n   Hostile: target nested 2-3 shadow roots deep (each level has siblings next to the host that must be hidden); target is a direct child of a shadow root; a sibling in the shadow root that already has aria-hidden=\"true\" before the call (must stay hidden after restore, refcount 0 rule); an element with role=\"row\" in a shadow root (cells inside also hidden, as in light DOM).\n2. With the flag on, elements added after the call anywhere inside a target's shadow root or any shadow root enclosing a target (direct children of the root, or nested deeper inside a non-hidden ancestor like the host's path) are hidden. Nodes added inside a target stay visible. Nodes with `data-live-announcer=\"true\"` or `data-react-aria-top-layer=\"true\"` (added later, or present at call time, inside those shadow roots) stay visible, exactly as in the light DOM.\n   Hostile: node added to a shadow root that is several levels up from the target; node added inside an already hidden shadow sibling (no extra refcount / no double hide); live announcer added inside the shadow root.\n3. Targets can live in different shadow roots (e.g. an input in shadow root A and its popover in shadow root B, or one in light DOM and one in a shadow root). Both stay visible with their host paths; everything else in both roots and the light DOM is hidden; additions in both roots are hidden.\n4. The returned cleanup restores every element it hid (aria-hidden or inert removed, refcounts respected) in light DOM and in all shadow roots, and disconnects observation of every root: content added to any of those roots after cleanup is NOT hidden.\n5. Nested calls keep working with shadow roots: the observer stack's `disconnect()`/`observe()` stop/restart observation of all roots (light and shadow) for that call; after the inner call's cleanup the outer call again hides new content in shadow roots; refcounts across the two calls are correct. `keepVisible` still works for elements inside shadow roots. `shouldUseInert` option works for shadow-root elements too.\n6. With the flag off (default), behavior is unchanged: shadow roots are not walked or observed (a sibling inside a shadow root next to a target stays without aria-hidden), and all existing tests pass unchanged. The flag-off shadow test must run before any `enableShadowDOM()` call in the file (the flag cannot be turned off).\n7. Conventions: implementation stays in packages/@react-aria/overlays/src/ariaHideOutside.ts, reusing @react-aria/utils shadow DOM helpers where they fit (`nodeContains`, `isShadowRoot`, etc.); flag read via `shadowDOM()` from `@react-stately/flags`, added to packages/@react-aria/overlays/package.json dependencies (same version range as other packages, \"^3.1.2\") with yarn.lock updated (`yarn install` offline/`--mode=update-lockfile` or editing the workspace entry so `yarn constraints`/`node scripts/lint-packages.js` pass). Tests go in packages/@react-aria/overlays/test/ariaHideOutside.test.js in a new `describe('ariaHideOutside with Shadow DOM')` that calls `enableShadowDOM()` in beforeAll, after a flag-off test. `yarn jest packages/@react-aria/overlays`, `yarn eslint packages/@react-aria/overlays`, and `yarn check-types` pass.\n\nCHECKS: while working: `yarn jest packages/@react-aria/overlays/test/ariaHideOutside.test.js`. At the end: `yarn jest packages/@react-aria/overlays packages/@react-aria/combobox packages/@react-aria/dnd`, `yarn eslint packages/@react-aria/overlays`, `yarn check-types`.\n\nReply exactly:\n{\"found\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"review\": <review number or null>, \"finding_quote\": \"<verbatim quote of the finding, or empty>\",\n  \"sent\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"handoff\": <handoff number or null>, \"handoff_quote\": \"<verbatim quote from the handoff, or 